"control_subclass.mst — Dolphin SUBCLASSES a real Win32 control (DolphinDart DD10).

 A control — EDIT, BUTTON, LISTBOX — is a window belonging to a class comctl
 registered, and its WndProc is what MAKES it that control: caret, selection,
 keyboard handling, all of it. Dolphin gets its own code into that stream the
 way every Windows program does — it SUBCLASSES, swapping the WndProc for its
 own and chaining to the original.

 This probe is the SUBSTRATE spike for that, ahead of the `UI.ControlView`
 wave: it exercises Dolphin's own `subclassWindow` shape

     dolphinWndProc := VM getWndProc.
     (oldProc := self setWndProc: dolphinWndProc) = dolphinWndProc
         ifFalse: [self oldWndProc: oldProc]

 and Dolphin's own `defaultWindowProcessing:wParam:lParam:`

     ^User32 callWindowProc: oldWndProc hWnd: handle msg: message
             wParam: wParam lParam: lParam

 on a real EDIT control, so the mechanism is proven before a class hierarchy
 is built on top of it. Both are copied from Dolphin's `UI.View` and
 `UI.ControlView` rather than invented; when those classes are translated they
 replace this probe and the shapes must still work unchanged.

 WHAT WOULD SILENTLY PASS WITHOUT THE PROBE BEING CAREFUL. A trampoline that
 routed nothing looks identical to a working control — the EDIT goes on
 editing, because its own procedure is still there. A trampoline that
 swallowed messages instead of chaining looks identical to a working IMAGE —
 the handler runs, and only the control is broken. So the gate asserts BOTH
 halves on the SAME message: the image saw it, and the text still arrived."
Object subclass: SubclassProbe [
    | hwnd oldWndProc seen lastMsg |

    "WM_SETTEXT. Routed here because it is the one message that proves both
     halves at once: the image can count it, and the EDIT control's own
     procedure is what actually STORES the text — so if the trampoline failed
     to chain, `text` would come back empty."
    SubclassProbe class >> wmSetText [ ^16r000C ]

    SubclassProbe class >> on: aParentHandle [
        ^self new setParent: aParentHandle
    ]

    setParent: aParentHandle [
        | cls |
        seen := 0.
        "Created through the generated floor, exactly as `WinControl` does —
         the control's window CLASS is comctl's, which is the whole point:
         nothing here registers a class of ours."
        cls := Utf16Buffer fromString: 'EDIT'.
        hwnd := [ User32
            createWindowEx: 0
            lpClassName: cls address
            lpWindowName: ''
            dwStyle: 16r50000000      "WS_CHILD | WS_VISIBLE"
            x: 0 y: 0 nWidth: 120 nHeight: 24
            hWndParent: aParentHandle
            hMenu: 0
            hInstance: 0
            lpParam: 0 ] ensure: [ cls free ].
        hwnd isNil ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'EDIT creation failed' ].
        ^self
    ]

    handle [ ^hwnd ]
    seen [ ^seen ]
    lastMsg [ ^lastMsg ]
    oldWndProc [ ^oldWndProc ]

    "Dolphin's `View>>subclassWindow`, verbatim in shape. The equality test is
     load-bearing: it is how Dolphin notices a window it has ALREADY
     subclassed and declines to record its own procedure as the one to chain
     to — which would be an infinite loop rather than a lost message."
    subclassWindow [
        | dolphinWndProc oldProc |
        dolphinWndProc := VM getWndProc.
        oldProc := User32
            setWindowLongPtr: hwnd
            nIndex: -4                 "GWLP_WNDPROC"
            dwNewLong: dolphinWndProc.
        oldProc = dolphinWndProc ifFalse: [ oldWndProc := oldProc ].
        "Tell the door what to chain to. Dolphin's VM needs no equivalent
         because it reflects EVERY message and lets the image chain; this door
         routes selectively, so a message it does not route has to reach the
         control's own procedure from C++ or the control stops working."
        Win32 mvpBindOldProc: hwnd proc: oldWndProc.
        ^oldProc
    ]

    unsubclassWindow [
        oldWndProc isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^User32 setWindowLongPtr: hwnd nIndex: -4 dwNewLong: oldWndProc
    ]

    "The flat hook the door uses when no Dolphin message map is installed.
     Answering nil means 'not mine' and the door chains — which is what this
     handler wants: it is COUNTING the message, not consuming it."
    onMessage: msg wParam: wp lParam: lp [
        seen := seen + 1.
        lastMsg := msg.
        ^nil
    ]

    "Dolphin's `ControlView>>defaultWindowProcessing:wParam:lParam:`, verbatim.
     The probe calls it directly to prove the chain is callable FROM THE IMAGE
     as well as from C++ — that is the path `ControlView` will use."
    defaultWindowProcessing: message wParam: wParam lParam: lParam [
        ^User32
            callWindowProc: oldWndProc
            hWnd: hwnd
            msg: message
            wParam: wParam
            lParam: lParam
    ]

    text [
        | n buf |
        n := User32 getWindowTextLength: hwnd.
        n = 0 ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        buf := Utf16Buffer new: (n + 1) * 2.
        User32 getWindowText: hwnd lpString: buf address nMaxCount: n + 1.
        ^buf stringValue
    ]
    text: aString [ ^User32 setWindowText: hwnd lpString: aString displayString ]

    destroy [ ^User32 destroyWindow: hwnd ]
]
